  "answer": "<p>The <b>correct approach</b> is to <b>copy your existing launch configuration, alter the instance type to c5.large in the new configuration, and then update your Auto Scaling Group to use this new configuration.</b> <br>This method (option 2) is required because launch configurations in AWS are immutable; you cannot edit instance types directly within an existing launch configuration. Making a copy allows you to select the new instance type and seamlessly assign the updated configuration to your Auto Scaling Group. This ensures all newly launched EC2 instances will use the desired c5.large type.<br><br><i>Option 1</i> suggests that the instance type can be changed directly within the Auto Scaling Group's console settings, but this is incorrect, as only the launch configuration or launch template can be specified, not edited in-place.<br><i>Option 3</i> describes stopping existing EC2 instances, changing their type, and restarting them. However, EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling Group are managed collectively, and manual changes to running instances are not maintained by the group; it may simply terminate and replace such instances to match the launch configuration.<br><i>Option 4</i> proposes a non-existent action called 'Change Instance Type' within the launch configuration. In reality, there is no way to edit an existing launch configuration—the option is not available.<br><br><b>Tip:</b> If you use launch templates instead of launch configurations, you can create a new version in a similar fashion, which provides more flexibility.<br></p>",
  "query": "<p>You are managing a Java application deployed via an Auto Scaling Group (ASG) in AWS. The application currently uses m5.large EC2 instances. After recent enhancements, the application is experiencing high CPU utilization, resulting in slower response times. You want to upgrade the EC2 instance type to c5.large to improve performance. <br>What is the optimal method to accomplish this instance type change?</p>",
